From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephano Cetola <stephanoc@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 8 Jul 2026 17:30:00 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] [BAREBOX] mmu: fix arch_remap_range permission-strip order and flush_cacheable_pages zero-size underflow

Two bugs in the MMU remapping path that together cause a crash during
NVMe PRP pool initialization when the pool is first sized from zero.

Bug 1: wrong ordering of arm_mmu_maybe_skip_permissions in
arch_remap_range.

When CONFIG_ARM_MMU_PERMISSIONS is not set, arm_mmu_maybe_skip_permissions()
promotes MAP_CACHED to MAP_CACHED_RWX. The previous code applied this
promotion BEFORE the maptype_is_compatible() check, so remapping back to
MAP_CACHED (e.g. in dma_free_coherent) always appeared incompatible and
spuriously triggered flush_cacheable_pages(). The check is supposed to
guard "are we switching away from cached?", stripping permissions first
corrupts it.

Fix: check maptype_is_compatible() on the original map_type, then strip
permissions for the PTE write.

Bug 2: size=0 underflow in flush_cacheable_pages.

flush_cacheable_pages() computes region_end as PAGE_ALIGN(start+size)-1.
With size=0 this underflows to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, causing the loop to
walk the entire 64-bit address space. Addresses beyond the hardware's
VA range alias to valid cacheable PTEs via find_pte()'s bit masking,
eventually provoking a translation fault when v8_flush_dcache_range is
called at an unmapped address.

Fix: return early when size=0.

Both bugs must be present for the crash to occur: bug 1 causes
flush_cacheable_pages to be called with size=0, bug 2 turns that into an
address-space walk.

Signed-off-by: Stephano Cetola <stephano@cetola.net>
---
 arch/arm/cpu/flush_cacheable_pages.h | 4 ++++
 arch/arm/cpu/mmu_64.c                | 8 ++++++--
 2 files changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm/cpu/flush_cacheable_pages.h b/arch/arm/cpu/flush_cacheable_pages.h
index c46a60071a..f690aa77d3 100644
--- a/arch/arm/cpu/flush_cacheable_pages.h
+++ b/arch/arm/cpu/flush_cacheable_pages.h
@@ -25,6 +25,10 @@ static void flush_cacheable_pages(void *start, size_t size)
 {
 	mmu_addr_t flush_start = ~0UL, flush_end = ~0UL;
 	mmu_addr_t region_start, region_end;
+
+	/* size=0 would compute region_end = PAGE_ALIGN(0)-1 = 0xFFFF...FFFF */
+	if (!size)
+		return;
 	size_t block_size;
 	mmu_addr_t *ttb;

diff --git a/arch/arm/cpu/mmu_64.c b/arch/arm/cpu/mmu_64.c
index 7f38473079..b365a6527c 100644
--- a/arch/arm/cpu/mmu_64.c
+++ b/arch/arm/cpu/mmu_64.c
@@ -286,11 +286,19 @@ static void early_remap_range(uint64_t addr, size_t size, maptype_t map_type)

 int arch_remap_range(void *virt_addr, phys_addr_t phys_addr, size_t size, maptype_t map_type)
 {
-	map_type = arm_mmu_maybe_skip_permissions(map_type);
-
+	/*
+	 * Check compatibility with the original map_type BEFORE stripping
+	 * permissions. arm_mmu_maybe_skip_permissions() maps MAP_CACHED to
+	 * MAP_CACHED_RWX when CONFIG_ARM_MMU_PERMISSIONS is disabled;
+	 * maptype_is_compatible(MAP_CACHED_RWX, MAP_CACHED) returns false,
+	 * which would spuriously trigger flush_cacheable_pages() even when
+	 * we're remapping back TO cached (e.g. in dma_free_coherent).
+	 */
 	if (!maptype_is_compatible(map_type, MAP_CACHED))
 		flush_cacheable_pages(virt_addr, size);

+	map_type = arm_mmu_maybe_skip_permissions(map_type);
+
 	return __arch_remap_range((uint64_t)virt_addr, phys_addr, (uint64_t)size, map_type, true);
 }
--
2.43.0

